from __future__ import annotations

from thinking_core import render_long


def coe_not_ai_department() -> str:
    body = '''<p>An AI Center of Excellence usually starts with a sensible intention: concentrate scarce expertise, create common methods and help the organization move faster. The risk appears later, when the same team becomes the place where every AI idea is expected to land.</p><p>At that point the CoE can look successful because demand is high. But the operating model is quietly becoming dependent on the center. Business teams bring problems, the CoE interprets them, the CoE finds resources, the CoE coordinates delivery, and the CoE remains close enough to keep the solution alive.</p><blockquote>A strong AI CoE should make the organization more capable of owning AI — not more dependent on the CoE to do AI for it.</blockquote><h2>Central expertise is useful. Central ownership is different.</h2><p>There are good reasons to centralize parts of AI. Architecture patterns, governance methods, specialist expertise, common tooling, literacy, portfolio visibility and reusable components all benefit from an enterprise view. A fragmented model where every function reinvents those foundations is expensive and inconsistent.</p><p>But business outcomes do not become enterprise-owned simply because a central team can deliver the technology. If a use case changes warranty decisions, sales activity, customer service, supply planning or another operating process, the accountable owner should sit close to that process.</p><p>The distinction I make is simple: the CoE can own the <strong>system for doing AI well</strong>. It should not automatically own every <strong>business result produced with AI</strong>.</p><h2>The trade-off is not centralize versus decentralize</h2><p>I do not see this as a binary choice. Fully centralized models can create consistency but also queues, distance from the business and weak local ownership. Fully distributed models can move close to the problem but duplicate expertise, create incompatible practices and make governance harder.</p><p>The better question is what should be common, what should stay close to the business and where the hand-off should happen.</p><p>For me, that usually points toward a federated model: a small enterprise capability that sets patterns, provides specialist support and maintains portfolio visibility, combined with business or product teams that own priorities, adoption and outcomes.</p><h2>Five responsibilities I expect from a useful AI CoE</h2><p><strong>1. Create a common lifecycle.</strong><br>Teams should understand how an AI opportunity moves from discovery to evidence, investment, production and review — without assuming that every use case needs the same level of process.</p><p><strong>2. Make decision rights explicit.</strong><br>Who can approve an experiment? Who owns business value? When does risk require central review? Who can stop or escalate a use case? Ambiguity here is more damaging than a missing template.</p><p><strong>3. Build reusable capability.</strong><br>Architecture patterns, governance methods, data practices, evaluation approaches, literacy and shared components should reduce repeated work across teams.</p><p><strong>4. Keep an enterprise portfolio view.</strong><br>The center should help leadership see where investment is going, which assumptions remain weak, where duplication exists and which initiatives deserve more attention — or less.</p><p><strong>5. Transfer ownership instead of collecting it.</strong><br>The sign of maturity is not how many initiatives the CoE controls. It is how many teams can responsibly own AI work because the organization now has clearer methods, guardrails and capability.</p><h2>The CoE should become less central to ordinary AI work</h2><p>This is the uncomfortable part. A mature CoE should make itself less necessary for routine decisions over time. Not irrelevant — the enterprise still needs common standards, specialist depth, portfolio governance and a place to handle novel or high-risk questions — but less involved in work that business and product teams should be capable of owning themselves.</p><p>That changes how I would measure a CoE. Number of projects delivered by the center is not enough. I would also look for shorter paths from idea to decision, stronger business ownership, more reusable patterns, fewer duplicated solutions, clearer evidence at investment gates and a growing ability for teams to operate responsibly without continuous central intervention.</p><div class="rule"><strong>My rule: build the CoE as an enabling system, not as the enterprise AI delivery department.</strong><p>The center should concentrate expertise where concentration creates value, then distribute capability and ownership where the business needs to operate it.</p></div>'''
    aside = '''<span>CoE design test</span><strong>What should the center still own after the first wave?</strong><div class="article-questions"><div>Common lifecycle and portfolio visibility</div><div>Enterprise standards and reusable patterns</div><div>Specialist support for novel or higher-risk work</div><div>Capability building and literacy</div><div>Not every business outcome or use case</div></div><p>If the CoE remains the default owner of ordinary AI delivery, the organization may have centralized activity without building distributed capability.</p>'''
    return render_long(
        "Point of view · AI Operating Model",
        "The AI CoE Should Not Become the Company’s AI Department",
        "A Center of Excellence should make the enterprise better at owning AI. If every use case still depends on the center, the organization has concentrated activity rather than built capability.",
        "17 Sep 2026 · 6 min read",
        body,
        aside,
    )


def strategy_to_value_framework() -> str:
    body = '''<p>Enterprise AI conversations often jump from strategy directly to use cases. That is where the middle of the operating system disappears.</p><p>A strategy can describe ambition. A use case can demonstrate possibility. Neither, by itself, explains how the organization will choose investments, govern different levels of risk, change real work or prove that value was created.</p><p>I use a simple five-part chain to keep those questions connected:</p><div class="flow" style="grid-template-columns:repeat(5,1fr);margin:32px 0"><div class="step"><span>01</span><strong>Strategy</strong></div><div class="step"><span>02</span><strong>Portfolio</strong></div><div class="step"><span>03</span><strong>Governance</strong></div><div class="step"><span>04</span><strong>Adoption</strong></div><div class="step"><span>05</span><strong>Value</strong></div></div><h2>1. Strategy — where should AI matter?</h2><p>Start with enterprise priorities, not the technology catalogue. The useful question is where better prediction, generation, automation or decision support could materially change an outcome the business already cares about.</p><h2>2. Portfolio — what deserves investment now?</h2><p>Strategy creates more opportunities than the organization can responsibly pursue. Portfolio discipline turns that abundance into choices: sequence initiatives, expose weak assumptions, fund learning where uncertainty is high and stop work that no longer earns its place.</p><h2>3. Governance — what must be true before the next commitment?</h2><p>Governance should scale with consequence. The point is not to impose the same gate on every experiment; it is to make ownership, evidence, risk, data responsibilities and escalation explicit as a use case asks for more reach or authority.</p><h2>4. Adoption — what actually changes in the work?</h2><p>An AI capability has not been adopted merely because people have access to it. Adoption becomes meaningful when a workflow, decision, role or behavior changes and the organization can observe that change.</p><h2>5. Value — did the changed work improve the outcome?</h2><p>Value is the final link, but it should be designed from the beginning. The chain needs to connect the use case to changed work, changed work to measurable operational evidence, and that evidence to a business outcome that leadership can defend.</p><blockquote>If one link is weak, the organization often compensates with more AI activity rather than fixing the operating problem.</blockquote><h2>The framework is a diagnostic, not a waterfall</h2><p>These five elements are connected, but they are not a rigid sequence. Portfolio evidence can force a strategy rethink. Governance can expose a missing owner. Adoption problems can show that the use case was designed around the technology rather than the workflow. Value evidence can change future portfolio choices.</p><p>That is why I use the chain as a management diagnostic. When an AI initiative is stuck, ask which link is actually weak before adding more delivery pressure.</p><div class="rule"><strong>Strategy gives direction. Portfolio creates choice. Governance creates confidence. Adoption changes work. Value proves the outcome.</strong><p>The practical discipline is keeping all five connected rather than treating them as separate AI workstreams.</p></div>'''
    aside = '''<span>Five-link diagnostic</span><strong>When AI activity is high but impact is weak, find the broken link.</strong><div class="article-questions"><div><strong>Strategy:</strong> Is the business priority clear?</div><div><strong>Portfolio:</strong> Are we making real choices?</div><div><strong>Governance:</strong> Are ownership and evidence explicit?</div><div><strong>Adoption:</strong> Has the work actually changed?</div><div><strong>Value:</strong> Can we trace the change to an outcome?</div></div><p>The framework is deliberately simple enough to use in an executive discussion without turning the discussion into an AI methodology exercise.</p>'''
    return render_long(
        "Framework · Enterprise AI",
        "Strategy → Portfolio → Governance → Adoption → Value",
        "A five-link management framework for connecting AI ambition to operating choices, changed work and measurable business outcomes.",
        "17 Sep 2026 · 4 min read",
        body,
        aside,
    )
